afk: rewrite subagent-driven-development for native isolated-subagent
parallelism. (Also touches implementer-prompt.md, task-reviewer-prompt.md,
and scripts/task-brief.)

Motivation: upstream assumes subagents share the working tree, so it
serializes tasks ("Never: Dispatch multiple implementation subagents in
parallel (conflicts)") and has implementers commit directly. On this
harness, file-editing subagents are dispatched isolated (copy-on-write
snapshot; changes parked on refs/omp/task/<id>; the orchestrator
cherry-picks — see profile/rules/isolated-task-merge.md), and the task
tool has no model parameter (agent types instead:
sonic/task/reviewer/scout/...). Isolation is OPT-IN per dispatch
(`isolated` is a required task-tool field; false = in-tree), so the
skill states the flag imperatively and the implementer template carries
it as an explicit tool parameter.

What must survive a rebase:
- Process = waves from the plan's Dependency Map: dispatch ALL ready
  tasks in parallel; review each task PRE-merge from
  its parked ref (review-package "$(git merge-base HEAD <ref>)" <ref>);
  merge only approved refs; wave review after a wave is fully merged;
  final whole-branch review unchanged.
- "Pre-Dispatch Baseline" section (project setup + clean test suite in
  the main workspace before wave 1) — moved here from
  using-git-worktrees, which is now a pure redirect and points back at
  this section.
- `isolated: true` stated as an explicit REQUIRED task-tool parameter
  on every implementer item (wave-scheduling bullet + implementer
  template field), never as an implicit property of the harness.
- File Handoffs are isolation-safe: briefs referenced by REPO-RELATIVE
  path (scripts/task-brief prints the relative form), reports at
  `local://task-N-report.md` (the parent-shared local:// root), and an
  explicit ban on absolute filesystem paths in dispatch prompts.
  Rationale: absolute parent paths in dispatches steered real
  implementers into reading/editing the SHARED checkout (isolation
  bypassed, ref empty, worktree dirtied — 2026-07-18 VibePN sessions);
  and the sdd workspace is self-gitignored, so an in-snapshot report
  would never reach the parked ref.
- Implementer template: "Work from: [directory]" dropped on purpose (it
  invited parent-tree paths); the template states the cwd IS the project
  root, forbids absolute paths into the original checkout, and names
  [REPORT_URI] (local://) as the only non-relative write target.
- Verdict policy: approved -> merge; fixable findings -> merge, then fix
  subagent on the merged state; fundamentally wrong -> delete the ref
  unmerged, re-dispatch the implementer with the findings.
- "Model Selection" stays translated to Agent Selection (sonic = cheap
  mechanical/transcription, task = default, reviewer = all reviews).
- Implementers never run git/jj; reports list files changed, not
  commits; ledger lines say "merged", not "commits".
- Dropped on purpose: "Never start on main/master" (jj describe-then-new
  flow has no branch checkouts) and the using-git-worktrees integration
  bullet (isolation comes from the isolated flag, not a worktree).
- Fix loop (upstream's 5 rounds + breaker/adjudication) is kept, but
  rounds dispatch a FRESH isolated fix subagent: a parked implementer
  cannot be resumed once the harness has captured its snapshot.
  Rounds 4-5 escalate the agent type (sonic -> task) instead of a model.
- Wave review section: after a wave is fully merged, one `reviewer` over
  the whole wave diff — the first look at parallel implementers' combined
  result — before the next wave.
- Reports stay at `local://task-N-report.md`, NOT in the per-plan
  `.superpowers/sdd/<plan>/` workspace (upstream puts them there): the
  workspace is self-gitignored, so an in-snapshot report never reaches
  the parked ref.
- review-package is always invoked over a parked ref:
  `review-package PLAN_FILE "$(git merge-base HEAD <ref>)" <ref>`, never
  BASE..HEAD recorded before dispatch.
- Finish step also asserts `git for-each-ref refs/omp/task` is empty.
- Fix loop is TEST-FIRST (2026-08-08 incident: a fix dispatch composed
  from the old fix-then-test prose let a fixer write tests after the
  fix): the fixer reproduces each finding as a failing test before
  touching production code; §4 links Template: fixer-prompt.md (the
  file ships in skills-sdd-fixer-prompt.patch); the pre-re-review check
  demands per-finding RED/GREEN evidence or `NOT TESTABLE: <why>`; the
  rationalization table carries the "write the test after" row. The
  string "The fixer fixes, re-runs the tests" must never return.

